Two pointer techniques

- you need to compare both ends
- data is sorted
- to avoid nested loop

This pattern is used when:
- Array is sorted
- You need pair / subarray / palindrome / remove duplicates
- Optimize from O(n²) → O(n)

left = 0
right = len(arr) - 1

O(n)

- Compare palindrome
- compare sorted array

Why does two-pointer technique work only on sorted arrays for sum problems?

Two-pointer technique relies on the sorted order to make decisions.
When the sum is too small, moving the left pointer increases the sum.
When the sum is too large, moving the right pointer decreases the sum.
This predictable behavior is only possible because the array is sorted.

Final one-liner:

“Two pointers work on sorted arrays because pointer movement has a predictable effect on the sum.”